y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n\n- $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/example-21795.json" "file_sha256" "f2ce489d032741677f088e83e2b77524e224fa32ca4e2eeb34babbc6a70bd722"} {"question" "e-21795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/example-21795.json" "file_sha256" "f2ce489d032741677f088e83e2b77524e224fa32ca4e2eeb34babbc6a70bd722"} {"question" "e-21800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny''(x) + 2y(x) = x,  \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n- Robin\n\n- Dirichlet\n\n- Neumann\n\n- Periodic" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/example-21800.json" "file_sha256" "66ce96a13391abf4922a949bf9c8c8e98bf70919222a1c26eae3869557050077"} {"question" "e-21800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\ny'(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a) = y(b), \\qquad y'(a)  = y'(b)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i,$ and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x = a = 0$ and $x = b = 2.$\n\n$$\ny(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/example-21800.json" "file_sha256" "66ce96a13391abf4922a949bf9c8c8e98bf70919222a1c26eae3869557050077"} {"question" "e-21796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0,  \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/c1e4a45a8051dccc92a71ca2cf0b3851cfb5c6c5c22e2fc416ebcc8aeba96740.png).\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/077041b590cf1030028330dae6748828deef81c3d8d6a1c70994a90407ac05a2.png) boundary conditions are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/50f935ce3a16bbb94697f74737b4bba66dc9ce01d6629e007ff64f46347c1ccb.png)\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/339c256afc594fe366f0fdcb008556ef43b33be529fed25c65e7faa077166be2.png)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/example-21796.json" "file_sha256" "e5c9c3c2f12997fd63d6a77706af73d2b750c7ce16fa26623c0e0df446c899dd"} {"question" "e-21796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\ny'(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i,$ and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., $\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0,$ the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/example-21796.json" "file_sha256" "e5c9c3c2f12997fd63d6a77706af73d2b750c7ce16fa26623c0e0df446c899dd"}] "usage" {"e-21795" [] "e-21800" [] "e-21796" []} "basis" 2182 "retractions" [[17592186151834 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n1. $y'' = x\\cos(x), \\quad y(2)=7$\n2. $y'' + 3y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n3. $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$"] [17592186151834 :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y$, then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)."] [17592186151836 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y''(x) + 2y(x) = x, \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n1. Robin\n2. Dirichlet\n3. Neumann\n4. Periodic"] [17592186151836 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Periodic:\n$y(a) = y(b), \\qquad y'(a) = y'(b)$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x = a = 0$ and $x = b = 2$.\n\n$$\ny(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions."] [17592186151835 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0, \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3$\n\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the $AAAAA$ boundary conditions are $AAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is $AAAAA$."] [17592186151835 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)\n$$\n\nOtherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0$.\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0$, the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}$.\n- The boundary conditions are\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$.\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$.\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
